"Captures an expert's scenario of process behavior using units of behavior, precedence links and explicit junction semantics.\n\nUse when: Document how a real operational scenario unfolds. Capture alternative and concurrent behavior during structured expert interviews.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns only functional inputs and controls, independent of scenarios.\n\nVisual grammar: Numbered units of behavior represent scenario activities. Precedence links constrain their occurrence order. AND, OR and exclusive-OR junctions distinguish branching and joining behavior.\n\nBuild: Name a scenario, viewpoint and start-to-finish boundary. Elicit the units of behavior and required precedence from experts. Add junctions and referents, documenting assumptions behind alternatives.\n\nCheck: Each behavior belongs to the selected scenario. Junction logic matches the intended possible occurrences. Referents resolve to documented content rather than unexplained off-page work.\n\nPitfalls: Treating every linked behavior as immediately consecutive. Mixing incompatible scenarios without distinguishing their conditions.\n\nExample: A maintenance scenario describes isolate equipment, inspect components and either repair or replace them before a controlled return to service.\n\nAlternatives: IDEF0 function model, BPMN process."
